>>[I think special class-specific properties should be acquirable by 
>> other classes--- classes should only determine the initial conditions.]

>[David Hairston]
> well, yes, but ... priests alone should be able to identify blessings
> and nurses shouldn't attack healers and samurais should be better martial
> artists and rogues should be better thieves and only females should be
> able to lay eggs and wizards should be better magic-users and ...

I happily concede females exclusive rights to lay eggs, on the grounds
of common sense.  But that is irrelevant, as feminitity is already an
*acquirable* property in nethack by means other than sex=female at
startup.  Nurses not attacking healers is pretty trivial, albeit a bit
at variance with observed behaviour in the case of specific arrogant
doctors:-)

However, I object to the other cases cited--- surely by virtue of
religious devotion a character could achieve ordination?  Its less
flashy than HoE.  It could be granted on #offer, or perhaps by a
vigil--- a long uninterruptable prayer during which the nasties can
creep up and eat you.

The other cases all assume some pre-dungeon training.  Well fair
enough, but if we are letting barbarians cast spells surely we can let
characters find a "Manual of Lockpicking" and embark on a bit of self
improvement?  There could be other new books such as "Bonewitz on
Wizardry" and "The True Path to Martial Excellence".

[From another article]
> btw, shouldn't the player get to choose starting alignment since
> there are alternate strategies for playing each alignment or perhaps
> to be more traditional about it, shouldn't starting alignment be
> random, in some/most/all cases?

I suggest that alignment should be an option.  Yes, samurai were
generally what you might call "lawful", but I expect there were some
deviant individuals:-)  Please give players the choice, but leave the
current alignment configuration as the default.

